  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the compressor do in a gas turbine?",
      "a": "It squeezes incoming air to around 20 times normal pressure before the fuel burns in it. The hot, high-pressure gas then expands through the turbine with great force. The compressor uses over half of the turbine's own power.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a gas turbine squeeze the air?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the heat recovery boiler do?",
      "a": "It is a huge boiler with no flame of its own. Hot exhaust from the gas turbine flows past bundles of water tubes, boiling the water into steam for the steam turbine.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a heat recovery steam generator?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is combined cycle more efficient?",
      "a": "A gas turbine on its own throws away very hot exhaust. Using that heat to run a steam turbine turns waste into extra electricity, so about 60 percent of the fuel's energy is used instead of about 38 percent.",
      "asks": [
        "How efficient is a combined-cycle gas plant?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are gas power stations useful for the grid?",
      "a": "Gas turbines can change their output quickly, and simple open-cycle ones reach full power in about 10 to 30 minutes, much faster than coal or nuclear. So gas plants fill gaps when demand jumps or the wind drops.",
      "asks": [
        "How fast can a gas power station start?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Does a gas power station release carbon dioxide?",
      "a": "Yes. Burning natural gas makes carbon dioxide and water, but a combined-cycle plant emits roughly half as much carbon dioxide as a coal station for each unit of electricity.",
      "asks": [
        "Is gas power cleaner than coal?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The best combined-cycle gas plants reach a little over 60 percent efficiency, the highest of any fuel-burning power station.",
    "In a typical combined-cycle plant, about two thirds of the electricity comes from the gas turbine and one third from the steam turbine."
  ],
